Welcome to Module 2. Over the next four weeks, we're going to take the fundamentals you've been building and put them to work on real projects. Each week, we'll build something together in class, and then for homework you'll build your own version from scratch.

We're starting with one of the most common and useful things news developers build: a database explorer. That's a searchable, filterable page that lets readers dig through records on their own. Newsrooms use them to put public data directly in the hands of their audience.

## Part 1: What is a database explorer?

You've probably used a database explorer without thinking about it. They're everywhere in journalism.

ProPublica's [Nonprofit Explorer](https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/) lets you search the tax filings of every nonprofit in America. The Marshall Project's [The Next to Die](https://www.themarshallproject.org/next-to-die) tracks upcoming executions. WNYC built [a tool](https://projects.wnyc.org/rent-stabilized/) that lets you check whether your building is rent-stabilized. The New York Times publishes [a database of police misconduct records](https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2023/10/20/us/nypd-misconduct-discipline-records.html).

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/propublica-nonprofits.png"
  alt="ProPublica's Nonprofit Explorer showing a search interface with a text input and results listing nonprofit organizations"
  chromeTitle="Nonprofit Explorer"
  chromeUrl="https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

What these tools have in common is a simple pattern: a list page where you can search and filter, and a detail page where you can drill into an individual record. That's what we're going to build today.

### Our dataset

We'll be working with lead paint violation data from the New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development, known as HPD. Under New York City law, landlords must address lead paint hazards in any apartment where a child under six lives. When an HPD inspector finds a lead paint hazard and the landlord fails to correct it, the agency issues a lead paint violation — one of the most serious housing code violations a building owner can receive.

Our dataset covers the Bronx, the borough with the highest concentration of open lead paint violations in the city.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/hpd-open-data.png"
  alt="The HPD Housing Maintenance Code Violations dataset on the NYC Open Data portal, showing a table of violation records"
  chromeTitle="Housing Maintenance Code Violations"
  chromeUrl="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Housing-Development/Housing-Maintenance-Code-Violations/wvxf-dwi5"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

The raw dataset on NYC Open Data contains millions of individual violation records going back to 2010. That's far too much data for a static site to handle. So I've prepared a smaller file for class that filters to open lead paint violations and rolls them up at the building level. Instead of one row per violation, it's one row per building, with a count of how many open lead paint violations each building has and the individual violation records nested inside.

The editorial question we're answering: **Which Bronx buildings have the most unresolved lead paint violations right now?**

### Set up your project

By now, this part should feel like muscle memory.

Open your browser and navigate to our template repository at <a href="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template" target="_blank">github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template</a>. Click "Use this template" and create a new repository called `bronx-lead-paint`. Make sure "Public" is selected, then click "Create repository."

Clone the repository to your computer and open it in Visual Studio Code. Open a terminal and run:

```bash
npm install
```

Then start the development server:

```bash
npm run dev
```

Visit [localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173) to make sure it's working. You should see the familiar template page.

## Part 2: Loading the data

I'm going to give you a file called `bronx-lead-paint.json`. Download it from the link I've posted in the class chat and save it in your project at `src/lib/data/bronx-lead-paint.json`.

You'll need to create the `data` folder inside `src/lib/` first. Right-click on `src/lib/` in the Explorer sidebar and select "New Folder." Name it `data`. Then move or save the JSON file there.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/vscode-data-folder.png"
  alt="VS Code Explorer showing the src/lib/data/ folder with bronx-lead-paint.json inside it"
  showChrome={false}
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Before we do anything with this data, let's look at it. Open `bronx-lead-paint.json` in VS Code and scroll through it. You'll see an array of objects, each representing a building. Here's what one looks like:

```json
{
  "id": "34291",
  "address": "1520 GRAND CONCOURSE",
  "zip": "10457",
  "violationCount": 12,
  "latestDate": "2026-02-14",
  "lat": 40.8441,
  "lng": -73.9106,
  "violations": [
    {
      "description": "SECTION 27-2056 HMC: ABATE THE LEAD-BASED PAINT HAZARD CONSISTING OF PEELING PAINT SURFACES IN THE ENTIRE APARTMENT, 2ND STORY, APT 2B",
      "date": "2026-02-14"
    },
    {
      "description": "SECTION 27-2056.6 HMC: CORRECT HAZARDOUS CONDITION CONSISTING OF DETERIORATED LEAD-BASED PAINT SURFACES, WINDOW SILLS AND FRAMES, 3RD STORY, APT 3A",
      "date": "2026-01-22"
    }
  ]
}
```

Each building has a handful of top-level fields — `id`, `address`, `zip`, `violationCount`, `latestDate`, `lat`, and `lng` — plus a `violations` array containing the individual lead paint violation records with their descriptions and dates.

The descriptions follow HPD's formal statutory language, but they make the hazard concrete: peeling paint surfaces, deteriorated window sills, a child in the apartment. These are the conditions that make lead poisoning a risk for young tenants.

Now let's load the data into our page. Open `src/routes/+page.svelte`. Clear out the existing template content so we're starting from a clean slate. Replace everything with:

```svelte
<script>
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx-lead-paint.json';

  console.log(buildings.length, 'buildings loaded');
  console.log(buildings[0]);
</script>

<h1>Bronx Lead Paint Violations</h1>
<p>{buildings.length} buildings with open lead paint violations</p>
```

Save the file, then open your browser's developer console (right-click anywhere on the page, select "Inspect," and click the "Console" tab). You should see the number of buildings and the first record logged out.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-7/console-log.png"
  alt="Browser developer console showing the number of buildings loaded and the first record object expanded to show its fields"
  chromeTitle="Bronx Lead Paint Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

The `import` statement at the top is how SvelteKit loads a JSON file. The `$lib` path points to your `src/lib/` folder, so the full path resolves to the file you just saved. Once imported, `buildings` is a regular JavaScript array that you can loop through, filter, sort, and display.

Remove the `console.log` lines once you've confirmed the data is loading. They were just for testing.

## Part 3: Building the list

Now let's display the buildings on the page. We'll use the `{#each}` loop you learned in a previous class to iterate through the array and render a card for each building.

This is where working like a real newsroom developer starts to pay off. Our template already includes a `Card` component — a styled wrapper that turns any content into a clickable linked card. Rather than writing card markup from scratch, we can reach for it directly.

Update your script block to import `Card` and a helper called `base`:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="3-4"}
<script>
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx-lead-paint.json';
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
  import Card from '$lib/components/Card.svelte';

  let sorted = [...buildings].sort((a, b) => b.violationCount - a.violationCount);
</script>
```

The [`.sort()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/sort) method takes a comparison function. This one says: "compare two buildings by subtracting `a`'s count from `b`'s count." When the result is positive, `b` comes first. That gives us descending order — the worst buildings at the top.

`base` is a SvelteKit helper that holds the URL prefix for your site on GitHub Pages. Your project will live at a URL like `yourusername.github.io/bronx-lead-paint/` — not at the root of a domain — so any links between your pages need to include that prefix to work correctly.

Now replace everything below your headline with an `{#each}` loop that renders a `Card` for each building:

```svelte
<h1>Bronx Lead Paint Violations</h1>
<p>{sorted.length} buildings with open lead paint violations</p>

{#each sorted as building}
  <Card href="{base}/building/{building.id}">
    <h3>{building.address}</h3>
    <p>{building.zip} · {building.violationCount} lead paint violations</p>
  </Card>
{/each}
```

`Card` wraps whatever you put inside it in a styled, clickable link. The `href` prop sets where it links. The content between the opening and closing tags — the address, zip code, and count — is entirely up to you. Save and check your browser — you should see a sorted, linked list of building cards.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/building-cards.png"
  alt="A styled list of building cards showing addresses, zip codes, and lead paint violation counts, sorted with the highest counts first, each card linking to a detail page"
  chromeTitle="Bronx Lead Paint Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

## Part 4: Search and filters

A list of hundreds of buildings isn't very useful without a way to narrow it down. Let's add a search box and a filter dropdown.

We'll use the same reactive tools you learned in [Week 4](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-4/): `$state` for tracking what the user has typed or selected, `$derived` for automatically recalculating the filtered list, and `bind:value` for connecting inputs to state variables.

### Adding a search input

Start by adding a `$state` variable for the search term and a `$derived` variable for the filtered results. Replace the `sorted` variable and swap in our template components:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="4-5,7-14"}
<script>
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx-lead-paint.json';
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
  import Card from '$lib/components/Card.svelte';
  import SearchInput from '$lib/components/SearchInput.svelte';

  let search = $state('');

  let filtered = $derived(
    buildings
      .filter(b => b.address.toLowerCase().includes(search.toLowerCase()))
      .sort((a, b) => b.violationCount - a.violationCount)
  );
</script>
```

The `$derived` expression does two things. First, it filters the buildings array to only include those whose address contains the search term. The [`.toLowerCase()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String/toLowerCase) calls on both sides make the search case-insensitive, so typing "grand" will match "GRAND CONCOURSE." Second, it sorts the results by violation count, just as before.

Whenever `search` changes — because the user typed something — Svelte automatically re-runs the `$derived` expression and updates everything on the page that depends on it.

Now add a `SearchInput` above the `{#each}` loop. Our template's `SearchInput` component is a styled text field that wires up `bind:value` exactly like a raw `<input>` would:

```svelte
<SearchInput bind:value={search} placeholder="Search by address..." />

<p>{filtered.length} buildings</p>

{#each filtered as building}
  <Card href="{base}/building/{building.id}">
    <h3>{building.address}</h3>
    <p>{building.zip} · {building.violationCount} lead paint violations</p>
  </Card>
{/each}
```

Save and try it. Type an address into the search box and watch the list narrow in real time.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/search-working.png"
  alt="The building list with a search input at the top. The user has typed 'grand' and the list shows only buildings on Grand Concourse."
  chromeTitle="Bronx Lead Paint Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

### Adding a zip code filter

Now let's add a dropdown that filters by zip code. First, we need a list of all the unique zip codes in the dataset. We can build it with a JavaScript technique that extracts one value from every record and removes duplicates. Add `Dropdown` to your imports and build the zips list:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="6,8"}
<script>
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx-lead-paint.json';
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
  import Card from '$lib/components/Card.svelte';
  import SearchInput from '$lib/components/SearchInput.svelte';
  import Dropdown from '$lib/components/Dropdown.svelte';

  let zips = [...new Set(buildings.map(b => b.zip))].sort();
  let search = $state('');
  let zipFilter = $state('all');

  let filtered = $derived(
    buildings
      .filter(b => {
        let matchesSearch = b.address.toLowerCase().includes(search.toLowerCase());
        let matchesZip = zipFilter === 'all' || b.zip === zipFilter;
        return matchesSearch && matchesZip;
      })
      .sort((a, b) => b.violationCount - a.violationCount)
  );
</script>
```

That `zips` line does a lot in a small space, so let's unpack it. Reading from the inside out:

- `buildings.map(b => b.zip)` creates a new array containing just the zip code from every building. If there are 500 buildings, this produces an array of 500 zip codes, many of them duplicates.
- `new Set(...)` converts that array into a [Set](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Set), which is a special JavaScript data structure that automatically removes duplicates.
- `[...` `]` spreads the Set back into a regular array, so we can call `.sort()` on it.
- `.sort()` puts the zip codes in order.

The result is a clean list of unique zip codes like `["10451", "10452", "10453", ...]` that we can use to populate a dropdown menu.

We also added a `zipFilter` state variable with a default value of `'all'`, and updated the `$derived` filter to check both conditions. A building must match the search text **and** the zip code filter to appear in the results.

Now swap in the template's `Dropdown` component next to `SearchInput`. `Dropdown` accepts a `bind:value` binding and an `options` array — each option is an object with a `value` and a `label`:

```svelte
<div class="controls">
  <SearchInput bind:value={search} placeholder="Search by address..." />

  <Dropdown
    bind:value={zipFilter}
    options={[
      { value: 'all', label: 'All zip codes' },
      ...zips.map(z => ({ value: z, label: z }))
    ]}
  />
</div>

<p>{filtered.length} buildings</p>
```

The `options` array starts with the "All zip codes" catch-all, then spreads in the dynamic list using the spread operator (`...`). We're building the options array right in the template — no extra variable needed.

Add a `<style>` block at the bottom of `+page.svelte` to lay the controls out side by side:

```css
.controls {
  display: flex;
  gap: 1rem;
  margin: 1rem 0;
}
```

Save and check your browser. You should now be able to search by address, filter by zip code, or combine both. The count above the list updates automatically as you change the filters.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/filters-working.png"
  alt="The building list with both a SearchInput and a Dropdown. A zip code is selected and the count shows a smaller number of filtered results."
  chromeTitle="Bronx Lead Paint Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Try selecting a zip code and then typing in the search box. The filters compose — they both apply at the same time. This is the same reactive pattern you used in Week 4, now applied to a real dataset with real consequences.

## Part 5: Detail pages

So far, everything lives on a single page. But a good database explorer lets you click into individual records and see their full story. That's what we'll build now.

In SvelteKit, every page on your site is a file inside the `src/routes/` folder. Up until now, we've only used `src/routes/+page.svelte`, which is the homepage. But SvelteKit can create pages at any URL by adding folders and files inside `src/routes/`.

We want a separate page for every building, at a URL like `/building/34291`, where the number is the building's ID. To do this, we'll use a SvelteKit feature called [dynamic routes](https://kit.svelte.dev/docs/routing#page).

### Creating the route

Create a new folder structure inside `src/routes/`:

```
src/routes/building/[id]/
```

The square brackets around `[id]` are the key. They tell SvelteKit that this part of the URL is a variable — it could be any value. When someone visits `/building/34291`, SvelteKit will load this route and set `id` to `"34291"`.

Inside that folder, create two files. The first is `+page.js`. This is a new type of file we haven't used before. It's called a [load function](https://kit.svelte.dev/docs/load), and its job is to prepare data before the page renders.

Create `src/routes/building/[id]/+page.js`:

```js
import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx-lead-paint.json';

export function load({ params }) {
  const building = buildings.find(b => b.id === params.id);
  return { building };
}

export function entries() {
  return buildings.map(b => ({ id: b.id }));
}
```

There are two functions here. Let's take them one at a time.

The `load` function receives a `params` object containing the dynamic parts of the URL. Since our folder is called `[id]`, `params.id` will contain whatever value appears in the URL. We use JavaScript's [`.find()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/find) method to locate the matching building in our dataset, and return it as an object.

The `entries` function serves a different purpose. Because our site is a static site — all the pages are built ahead of time, not generated on-the-fly — SvelteKit needs to know every possible page that could exist. The `entries` function provides that list. It maps over all the buildings and returns an array of objects, each containing an `id` value. When you build the site, SvelteKit will loop through these and generate a separate HTML page for each one.

### Creating the detail page

Now create the page that will display the building's details. Create `src/routes/building/[id]/+page.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
  import HeroTopper from '$lib/components/HeroTopper.svelte';
  import Card from '$lib/components/Card.svelte';
  import MethodologyBox from '$lib/components/MethodologyBox.svelte';
  let { data } = $props();
  let building = data.building;
</script>

<a href="{base}/">← Back to all buildings</a>

<HeroTopper
  headline={building.address}
  deck="{building.violationCount} open lead paint violations · ZIP {building.zip}"
  byline="NYC HPD Data"
  date={building.latestDate}
/>

<h2>Violations</h2>

{#each building.violations as violation}
  <Card href="https://hpdonline.nyc.gov/hpdonline/provide-information">
    <p class="description">{violation.description}</p>
    <p class="date">{violation.date}</p>
  </Card>
{/each}

<MethodologyBox>
  <p>
    Violation records come from the
    <a href="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Housing-Development/Housing-Maintenance-Code-Violations/wvxf-dwi5" target="_blank">NYC Department of Housing Preservation and Development</a>
    via NYC Open Data. Only open lead paint violations are shown.
    Click any violation card to look up the building on
    <a href="https://hpdonline.nyc.gov/hpdonline/provide-information" target="_blank">HPD Online</a>.
  </p>
</MethodologyBox>
```

The key line is the first one in the script block: `let { data } = $props()`. When a page has a `+page.js` load function, SvelteKit passes whatever it returns as a `data` prop. So `data.building` is the building object we found in the load function.

Notice that we're reusing the same `HeroTopper`, `Card`, and `MethodologyBox` components from the list page. This is the payoff of a shared component shelf: the same building block that creates a bold list-page header also makes individual building pages feel polished and consistent — just like ProPublica's [Nonprofit Explorer](https://projects.propublica.org/nonprofits/), where every organization page is built from the same template with different data.

`HeroTopper` on the detail page works without a `lottieSrc` — leave that prop off and the layout adjusts to fill the full width with just the text. `Card` here links each violation record to the HPD Online lookup page, giving readers a path to verify what they're reading. That's a small but meaningful act of transparency.

Add some styles at the bottom:

```css
.description {
  margin: 0 0 0.25rem;
  line-height: 1.4;
}

.date {
  font-size: 0.875rem;
  color: var(--color-dark-gray);
  margin: 0;
}
```

Save the file.

### Linking to detail pages

Because the `Card` component already renders each entry as a link, the list is wired up automatically. The `href` we passed in Part 3 — `{base}/building/{building.id}` — points directly to the dynamic route we just created. Save and click on any card to test it.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/detail-page.png"
  alt="A detail page for a specific Bronx building showing the address as a headline, a summary box with violation count, zip code, and latest inspection date, followed by a list of individual violations with descriptions and dates"
  chromeTitle="1520 GRAND CONCOURSE"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173/building/34291"
