/>

Click the "Back to all buildings" link at the top to return to the list. Your search and filter state will reset — that's expected for a static site. Each page load starts fresh.

## Part 6: Adding a locator map

Each building in our dataset has latitude and longitude coordinates. Let's use them to add a small map to the detail page that shows where the building is located.

Our template includes a `LocatorMap` component that embeds an [OpenStreetMap](https://www.openstreetmap.org/) view centered on any set of coordinates. It uses an iframe — a way to embed one web page inside another — and constructs the URL automatically from the coordinates you pass in. No extra packages required.

Open `src/routes/building/[id]/+page.svelte` and add the import:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="4"}
<script>
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
  import HeroTopper from '$lib/components/HeroTopper.svelte';
  import LocatorMap from '$lib/components/LocatorMap.svelte';
  import Card from '$lib/components/Card.svelte';
  import MethodologyBox from '$lib/components/MethodologyBox.svelte';
  let { data } = $props();
  let building = data.building;
</script>
```

Place the component after the `HeroTopper` and before the violations list. `LocatorMap` accepts two props — `lat` and `lng` — and renders an OpenStreetMap embed centered on those coordinates with a marker at the exact spot:

```svelte
<LocatorMap lat={building.lat} lng={building.lng} />
```

Save and visit a building's detail page. You should see a small interactive map showing the building's location in the Bronx.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/locator-map.png"
  alt="The detail page now includes a small embedded OpenStreetMap showing a red marker at the building's location, with the surrounding Bronx streets visible"
  chromeTitle="1520 GRAND CONCOURSE"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173/building/34291"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

In a couple of weeks, we'll learn how to build full interactive maps from scratch using a library called MapLibre. For now, this simple embed is enough to orient the reader.

## Part 7: Editorial polish

The data is loaded, the search works, and the detail pages are in place. But right now the page is just a database. It needs a frame — a bold editorial header and a source credit at the bottom — to make it a piece of journalism.

ProPublica, The Marshall Project, and other leading newsrooms rarely start a database explorer with a plain `<h1>`. They lead with a designed topper: large type, a contextual summary, a byline, and often a looping illustration that sets the tone before the data appears. Our template includes a `HeroTopper` component that brings this pattern within reach.

### Get a Lottie animation

`HeroTopper` uses [Lottie](https://lottiefiles.com/) — a file format for lightweight vector animations — to display a looping illustration alongside your headline.

Go to [lottiefiles.com](https://lottiefiles.com/) and create a free account. Search for an animation that fits your story. For a lead paint project, something like a building, a child, or a paint brush animation works well. When you find one you like, click "Download" and choose "Lottie JSON." Save the file as `animation.json` in your project's `static/` folder.

### Install lottie-web

`HeroTopper` renders the animation using [lottie-web](https://www.npmjs.com/package/lottie-web), the standard library for playing Lottie files in a browser. Install it in your project:

```bash
npm install lottie-web
```

### Add the hero topper

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte` and add the import:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="2"}
<script>
  import HeroTopper from '$lib/components/HeroTopper.svelte';
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx-lead-paint.json';
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
  import SearchInput from '$lib/components/SearchInput.svelte';
  import Dropdown from '$lib/components/Dropdown.svelte';
  import Card from '$lib/components/Card.svelte';
  // ... rest of your script
</script>
```

Replace the `<h1>` at the top of the template with the component:

```svelte
<HeroTopper
  headline="Bronx Buildings with Open Lead Paint Violations"
  deck="Using HPD inspection records to surface unresolved lead paint hazards in New York's most violation-prone borough"
  byline="Your Name"
  date="March 2026"
  lottieSrc="/animation.json"
/>
```

The `deck` prop is a one- or two-sentence summary — think of it as the lede you'd write for a story. `byline` and `date` appear in smaller type below the deck. `lottieSrc` is the path to the JSON file you placed in `static/`.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/hero-topper.png"
  alt="The database explorer with a bold full-width hero header. On the left: a large headline in white type on a dark background, a summary paragraph, and a byline. On the right: a looping Lottie animation of a building."
  chromeTitle="Bronx Buildings with Open Lead Paint Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

### Add a methodology box

Readers trust data journalism more when they can see exactly where the numbers came from. The template includes a `MethodologyBox` component for just this purpose — a structured, consistently styled block that explains the data's origin, how it was processed, and any caveats a reader should know.

Add the import:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="2"}
<script>
  import MethodologyBox from '$lib/components/MethodologyBox.svelte';
  import HeroTopper from '$lib/components/HeroTopper.svelte';
  // ... rest of your imports
</script>
```

At the bottom of the page, after the `{#each}` loop, drop in the component with your source information as children:

```svelte
<MethodologyBox>
  <p>
    Data comes from the <a href="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Housing-Development/Housing-Maintenance-Code-Violations/wvxf-dwi5" target="_blank">NYC Department of Housing Preservation and Development</a>
    via NYC Open Data. This page shows open lead paint violations for buildings in the Bronx.
    Records were filtered to lead paint violations under Section 27-2056 HMC and aggregated
    at the building level as of March 2026.
  </p>
  <p>
    For more information about a specific building's violations, visit
    <a href="https://hpdonline.nyc.gov/hpdonline/provide-information" target="_blank">HPD Online</a>.
  </p>
</MethodologyBox>
```

The component renders an "About the data" box with a bordered left accent and a collapsed/expandable toggle so it does not visually compete with the data itself. You can put any mix of paragraphs, lists, and links between its tags — it accepts open-ended children.

